From: oplus-sukisu_kernel maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] sched/fair: re-apply the BORE parity guard for 6.12.23

The pick_eevdf() hunk in bore_6.12.patch is written against 6.12.44+, where
the run-to-parity check reads:

	if (curr && protect && protect_slice(curr))
		return curr;

OnePlus's sm8850 16.0.0 tree (6.12.23) still has the older form:

	if (sched_feat(RUN_TO_PARITY) && curr && protect_slice(curr))
		return curr;

so that hunk rejects. This re-applies the same guard against the older form.
The guard is identical in intent: when burst parity is configured, a task
that has used its slice is not allowed to run to parity, so it does not get
an extra slice ahead of the tasks behind it.

The build applies this only when kernel/sched/fair.c.rej exists, and only
after the main BORE patch has run.

diff --git a/kernel/sched/fair.c b/kernel/sched/fair.c
--- a/kernel/sched/fair.c
+++ b/kernel/sched/fair.c
@@ -1004,6 +1004,10 @@
 		curr = NULL;
 
 	if (sched_feat(RUN_TO_PARITY) && curr && protect_slice(curr))
+#ifdef CONFIG_SCHED_BORE
+		if (!(likely(sched_bore) && likely(sched_burst_parity_threshold) &&
+			sched_burst_parity_threshold < cfs_rq->nr_running))
+#endif // CONFIG_SCHED_BORE
 		return curr;
 
 	/* Pick the leftmost entity if it's eligible */
